# Line-ending policy for the generated tree (issue #699).
#
# `compile --all --emit` writes LF on every platform (the compiler emits bytes, and
# `determinism.write_text_lf` covers the projection lane). Without this file a
# contributor on `core.autocrlf=true` materialises the *tracked* publish tree as CRLF at
# checkout, so the next emit rewrites every file back to LF and `git status` reports the
# whole tree as modified while each file is byte-identical to its index blob. Measured on
# a real hub: 168 files, and `git checkout <branch>` refusing to switch.
#
# That is not cosmetic. The documented recovery -- hand-curating the regeneration commit
# to "skip line-ending-only noise" -- silently dropped genuinely new artifacts, and the
# drift gate caught it two commits later.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Stated explicitly for the generated and authored text the hub tracks, so the policy
# survives a future change to Git's `text=auto` heuristics.
*.sql   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.ttl   text eol=lf
*.rdf   text eol=lf
*.owl   text eol=lf
*.csv   text eol=lf
*.mmd   text eol=lf

# Binary, and load-bearing: a hub tracks source-sample extracts under
# `.import/sources/**` and client workbooks. Line-ending conversion would corrupt them.
*.parquet binary
*.xlsx    binary
*.xlsb    binary
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.zip     binary
*.pbix    binary
